import meta::pure::test::pct::*;

'''
The number of the histogram bucket a value falls into.

The range `low` to `high` is divided into `count` buckets of equal width, numbered `1` to `count`.
A value inside the range returns the number of its bucket; a value outside it returns a sentinel
instead of an error, so a whole column can be bucketed without pre-filtering. `0` means below the
range and `count + 1` means at or above it, which keeps the out-of-range rows visible in a group-by
rather than silently dropping them.

Buckets are half-open in the direction of travel, so a value sitting exactly on an internal edge
belongs to the bucket above it and every value maps to exactly one bucket. `low` itself is bucket
`1`; `high` itself is `count + 1`, not `count`.

The bounds may be given in either order. When `low` is greater than `high` the buckets run
downwards — bucket `1` is the one adjacent to `low` — and the half-open edge flips with them, so a
value on an internal edge again belongs to the bucket further from `low`.

Bucket edges are computed in floating point, so a value that sits exactly on an edge in decimal but
is not representable in binary may land in the neighbouring bucket. Where a decimal edge has to be
respected exactly, scale the value and the bounds to integers before bucketing.

The `count` must be positive and `low` must differ from `high`; either raises an error.

**Parameters**
- `value` — the number to place in a bucket.
- `low` — one end of the range.
- `high` — the other end of the range, which must differ from `low`.
- `count` — the number of equal-width buckets, a positive integer.

**Returns** the bucket number, `0` when the value is beyond the `low` end, or `count + 1` when it
is at or beyond the `high` end.

**Examples**
```pure
widthBucket(5.0, 0.0, 10.0, 5)     // 3
widthBucket(2.0, 0.0, 10.0, 5)     // 2    -- an internal edge belongs to the bucket above
widthBucket(0.0, 0.0, 10.0, 5)     // 1    -- low is in the first bucket
widthBucket(10.0, 0.0, 10.0, 5)    // 6    -- high is excluded
widthBucket(-5.0, 0.0, 10.0, 5)    // 0    -- below the range
widthBucket(99.0, 0.0, 10.0, 5)    // 6    -- above the range
widthBucket(9.0, 10.0, 0.0, 5)     // 1    -- reversed bounds count downwards
widthBucket(1.0, 10.0, 0.0, 5)     // 5
```

**See also** `widthBucket(Number[0..1], Number[1], Number[1], Integer[1]):Integer[0..1]` for a value
that may be missing, `binFloor(Integer[1], Integer[1]):Integer[1]`, `floor(Number[1]):Integer[1]`,
`round(Number[1]):Integer[1]`
'''
function <<PCT.function>> meta::pure::functions::math::widthBucket(value:Number[1], low:Number[1], high:Number[1], count:Integer[1]):Integer[1]
{
    assert($count > 0, 'count must be > 0');
    assert($low != $high, 'low must not equal high');

    let ascending = $low < $high;

    let span = if($ascending, | $high - $low, | $low - $high);
    let offset = if($ascending, | $value - $low, | $low - $value);

    if([
           pair(| $offset < 0,      | 0),
           pair(| $offset >= $span, | $count + 1)
       ],
       | (($offset * $count / $span)->floor()) + 1);
}

'''
As `widthBucket(Number[1], Number[1], Number[1], Integer[1]):Integer[1]`, for a value that may be
missing.

An absent `value` gives an absent result rather than a bucket number, so a column holding gaps can
be bucketed without pre-filtering and the missing entries stay distinguishable from the
out-of-range sentinels `0` and `count + 1` — both of which are real bucket answers about a real
value. Emptiness propagates rather than being folded into a bucket.

The bounds and `count` still have to be given. They are not checked when `value` is absent, so an
absent value yields an absent result even where a present one would have raised.

**Parameters**
- `value` — the number to place in a bucket, or nothing.
- `low` — one end of the range.
- `high` — the other end of the range, which must differ from `low`.
- `count` — the number of equal-width buckets, a positive integer.

**Returns** the bucket number as described for
`widthBucket(Number[1], Number[1], Number[1], Integer[1]):Integer[1]`, or nothing when `value` is
absent.

**Examples**
```pure
widthBucket([], 0.0, 10.0, 5)      // nothing
```

**See also** `widthBucket(Number[1], Number[1], Number[1], Integer[1]):Integer[1]`,
`isEmpty(Any[0..1]):Boolean[1]`
'''
function <<PCT.function>> meta::pure::functions::math::widthBucket(value:Number[0..1], low:Number[1], high:Number[1], count:Integer[1]):Integer[0..1]
{
    if($value->isEmpty(),
       | [],
       | widthBucket($value->toOne(), $low, $high, $count));
}

'''
A value inside the range gets the number of the bucket holding it, counting from `1`.
'''
function <<PCT.test>> meta::pure::functions::math::tests::widthBucket::testWidthBucketInRange<Z|y>(f:Function<{Function<{->Z[y]}>[1]->Z[y]}>[1]):Boolean[1]
{
    assertEq(2, $f->eval(|widthBucket(3.0, 0.0, 10.0, 5)));
    assertEq(3, $f->eval(|widthBucket(5.0, 0.0, 10.0, 5)));
}

'''
A value below the range is reported as `0` rather than raising, so it survives a group-by.
'''
function <<PCT.test>> meta::pure::functions::math::tests::widthBucket::testWidthBucketBelowRange<Z|y>(f:Function<{Function<{->Z[y]}>[1]->Z[y]}>[1]):Boolean[1]
{
    assertEq(0, $f->eval(|widthBucket(-5.0, 0.0, 10.0, 5)));
}

'''
A value above the range is reported as `count + 1`, the sentinel beyond the last bucket.
'''
function <<PCT.test>> meta::pure::functions::math::tests::widthBucket::testWidthBucketAboveRange<Z|y>(f:Function<{Function<{->Z[y]}>[1]->Z[y]}>[1]):Boolean[1]
{
    assertEq(6, $f->eval(|widthBucket(99.0, 0.0, 10.0, 5)));
}

'''
The `low` bound is inside the range and opens the first bucket.
'''
function <<PCT.test>> meta::pure::functions::math::tests::widthBucket::testWidthBucketAtLowBound<Z|y>(f:Function<{Function<{->Z[y]}>[1]->Z[y]}>[1]):Boolean[1]
{
    assertEq(1, $f->eval(|widthBucket(0.0, 0.0, 10.0, 5)));
}

'''
The `high` bound is excluded, so it falls to the out-of-range sentinel rather than the last bucket.
'''
function <<PCT.test>> meta::pure::functions::math::tests::widthBucket::testWidthBucketAtHighBound<Z|y>(f:Function<{Function<{->Z[y]}>[1]->Z[y]}>[1]):Boolean[1]
{
    assertEq(6, $f->eval(|widthBucket(10.0, 0.0, 10.0, 5)));
}

'''
A value exactly on an internal edge belongs to the bucket above it, never to both.
'''
function <<PCT.test>> meta::pure::functions::math::tests::widthBucket::testWidthBucketOnInternalBoundary<Z|y>(f:Function<{Function<{->Z[y]}>[1]->Z[y]}>[1]):Boolean[1]
{
    assertEq(2, $f->eval(|widthBucket(2.0, 0.0, 10.0, 5)));
    assertEq(5, $f->eval(|widthBucket(8.0, 0.0, 10.0, 5)));
}

'''
Integer arguments are accepted wherever a number is, since the bounds are continuous.
'''
function <<PCT.test>> meta::pure::functions::math::tests::widthBucket::testWidthBucketWithIntegers<Z|y>(f:Function<{Function<{->Z[y]}>[1]->Z[y]}>[1]):Boolean[1]
{
    assertEq(2, $f->eval(|widthBucket(17, 0, 100, 10)));
}

'''
The range need not start at zero, and negative bounds bucket the same way as positive ones.
'''
function <<PCT.test>> meta::pure::functions::math::tests::widthBucket::testWidthBucketNegativeRange<Z|y>(f:Function<{Function<{->Z[y]}>[1]->Z[y]}>[1]):Boolean[1]
{
    assertEq(3, $f->eval(|widthBucket(-5.0, -10.0, 0.0, 5)));
}

'''
A single bucket spans the whole range, so every value inside it is bucket `1`.
'''
function <<PCT.test>> meta::pure::functions::math::tests::widthBucket::testWidthBucketSingleBucket<Z|y>(f:Function<{Function<{->Z[y]}>[1]->Z[y]}>[1]):Boolean[1]
{
    assertEq(1, $f->eval(|widthBucket(5.0, 0.0, 10.0, 1)));
}

'''
Reversed bounds count downwards, so bucket `1` is the one adjacent to `low`.
'''
function <<PCT.test>> meta::pure::functions::math::tests::widthBucket::testWidthBucketDescending<Z|y>(f:Function<{Function<{->Z[y]}>[1]->Z[y]}>[1]):Boolean[1]
{
    assertEq(1, $f->eval(|widthBucket(9.0, 10.0, 0.0, 5)));
    assertEq(5, $f->eval(|widthBucket(1.0, 10.0, 0.0, 5)));
}

'''
With reversed bounds the `low` end is still included, so it opens the first bucket as before.
'''
function <<PCT.test>> meta::pure::functions::math::tests::widthBucket::testWidthBucketDescendingAtLowBound<Z|y>(f:Function<{Function<{->Z[y]}>[1]->Z[y]}>[1]):Boolean[1]
{
    assertEq(1, $f->eval(|widthBucket(10.0, 10.0, 0.0, 5)));
}

'''
The half-open edge flips with the direction, so an internal edge belongs to the bucket further from `low`.
'''
function <<PCT.test>> meta::pure::functions::math::tests::widthBucket::testWidthBucketDescendingOnInternalBoundary<Z|y>(f:Function<{Function<{->Z[y]}>[1]->Z[y]}>[1]):Boolean[1]
{
    assertEq(2, $f->eval(|widthBucket(8.0, 10.0, 0.0, 5)));
}

'''
The sentinels follow the direction too: past `low` is `0` and at or past `high` is `count + 1`.
'''
function <<PCT.test>> meta::pure::functions::math::tests::widthBucket::testWidthBucketDescendingOutOfRange<Z|y>(f:Function<{Function<{->Z[y]}>[1]->Z[y]}>[1]):Boolean[1]
{
    assertEq(0, $f->eval(|widthBucket(11.0, 10.0, 0.0, 5)));
    assertEq(6, $f->eval(|widthBucket(0.0, 10.0, 0.0, 5)));
}

'''
An absent value gives an absent result, not a bucket number and not an out-of-range sentinel.
'''
function <<PCT.test>> meta::pure::functions::math::tests::widthBucket::testWidthBucketAbsentValue<Z|y>(f:Function<{Function<{->Z[y]}>[1]->Z[y]}>[1]):Boolean[1]
{
    assertEmpty($f->eval(|widthBucket([], 0.0, 10.0, 5)));
}

function <<test.Test>> meta::pure::functions::math::tests::widthBucket::testWidthBucketOptionalValue():Boolean[1]
{
    let present = if(true, | 5.0, | []);
    let absent = if(false, | 5.0, | []);

    assertEquals(3, widthBucket($present, 0.0, 10.0, 5));
    assertEmpty(widthBucket($absent, 0.0, 10.0, 5));
}

function <<test.Test>> meta::pure::functions::math::tests::widthBucket::testWidthBucketAbsentValueSkipsValidation():Boolean[1]
{
    let absent = if(false, | 5.0, | []);

    assertEmpty(widthBucket($absent, 0.0, 10.0, 0));
    assertEmpty(widthBucket($absent, 5.0, 5.0, 3));
}

function <<test.Test>> meta::pure::functions::math::tests::widthBucket::testWidthBucketErrors():Boolean[1]
{
    assertError(|widthBucket(5.0, 0.0, 10.0, 0), 'count must be > 0');
    assertError(|widthBucket(5.0, 0.0, 10.0, -3), 'count must be > 0');
    assertError(|widthBucket(5.0, 5.0, 5.0, 3), 'low must not equal high');
}

function <<test.Test>> meta::pure::functions::math::tests::widthBucket::testWidthBucketEval():Boolean[1]
{
    assertEq(3, widthBucket_Number_1__Number_1__Number_1__Integer_1__Integer_1_->eval(5.0, 0.0, 10.0, 5));
    assertEq(0, widthBucket_Number_1__Number_1__Number_1__Integer_1__Integer_1_->eval(-5.0, 0.0, 10.0, 5));
}
